If $matbold(B) = matbold(P)^(-1) matbold(A) matbold(P)$, then

$ tr(matbold(A)) = tr(matbold(B)), quad det(matbold(A)) = det(matbold(B)), quad chi_(matbold(A))(t) = chi_(matbold(B))(t), $

so similar matrices represent the same linear map with respect to different bases.

For the particular case $matbold(D) = mat(lambda_1, 0, ..., 0; 0, lambda_2, ..., 0; dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; 0, 0, ..., lambda_n)$, this means a diagonalisable matrix satisfies

$ tr(matbold(A)) = sum_(i = 1)^n lambda_i, quad det(matbold(A)) = product_(i = 1)^n lambda_i, quad chi_(matbold(A))(t) = product_(i = 1)^n (lambda_i - t). $
